  <!-- Scientific Foundations Section -->
  <div class="mb-10 bg-surface-soft/40 border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8">
    <div class="mb-6">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-teal-600" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19.428 15.428a2 2 0 00-1.022-.547l-2.387-.477a6 6 0 00-3.86.517l-.318.158a6 6 0 01-3.86.517L6.05 15.21a2 2 0 00-1.806.547M8 4h8l-1 1v5.172a2 2 0 00.586 1.414l5 5c1.26 1.26.367 3.414-1.415 3.414H4.828c-1.782 0-2.674-2.154-1.414-3.414l5-5A2 2 0 009 10.172V5L8 4z"/>
        </svg>
        {d.scienceTitle}
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-1">{d.scienceSub}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-500"></span>
          Thermodynamics of Mechanical Vapor Recompression (MVR)
        </h4>
        <p>
          Unlike single-effect or multi-effect evaporators that condense secondary vapor in external barometric condensers (rejecting 2,260–2,400 kJ/kg of latent heat into cooling towers), MVR treats secondary vapor as a valuable thermodynamic working fluid.
        </p>
        <p>
          By mechanically compressing the boiled water vapor across a pressure ratio of 1.4 to 2.2 using high-efficiency centrifugal turbocompressors (isentropic efficiency η_is &gt; 82%), the saturation temperature of the steam is boosted by ΔT_lift = 4–12°C. When this recompressed vapor is redirected into the heating shell of the falling-film calandria, it condenses at T_cond = T_evap + ΔT_lift, transferring its full latent heat of vaporization back into the boiling feed solution. This cycle eliminates 90%+ of fossil boiler steam requirements, yielding an exceptional thermodynamic coefficient of performance (COP_th = 25–35).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-500"></span>
          Forward Osmosis Non-Thermal Pre-Concentration Mechanics
        </h4>
        <p>
          Forward Osmosis (FO) operates entirely under an osmotic pressure gradient (Δπ = π_draw - π_feed) across semi-permeable thin-film composite (TFC) or biomimetic aquaporin membranes with near-zero hydraulic pressure (P_hyd &lt; 1.5 bar).
        </p>
        <p>
          Because there is no external hydraulic compaction force forcing foulants into membrane pores, internal concentration polarization (ICP) and irreversible cake-layer fouling are dramatically suppressed. Sensitive dairy whey proteins (beta-lactoglobulin, immunoglobulins, lactoferrin) and volatile citrus juice esters/terpenes (limonene, citral) are completely shielded from high shear, pressure spikes, and temperatures above 45°C. The diluted draw solute is then re-concentrated inside the closed MVR loop, recovering pure deionized water while delivering high-Brix concentrates without vacuum pan fouling.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Technical Specifications Table -->
